1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,833
A man in a business suit hijacked a
passenger jet,

2
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:05,109
collected two hundred thousand dollars,

3
00:00:05,176 --> 00:00:07,272
and parachuted into a freezing night.

4
00:00:07,339 --> 00:00:11,192
More than fifty years later, the FBI still
cannot tell you his name.

5
00:00:16,792 --> 00:00:19,611
Almost every crime leaves something
behind.

6
00:00:19,677 --> 00:00:21,316
A fingerprint on a glass.

7
00:00:21,383 --> 00:00:22,759
A body in the ground.

8
00:00:22,826 --> 00:00:25,841
A name, a face, a witness, a paper trail
that,

9
00:00:25,908 --> 00:00:29,120
with enough time and enough people pulling
on it,

10
00:00:29,186 --> 00:00:30,956
eventually leads somewhere.

11
00:00:35,806 --> 00:00:37,153
This one led nowhere.

12
00:00:42,003 --> 00:00:44,760
On the night before Thanksgiving, in 1971,

13
00:00:44,827 --> 00:00:48,110
a quiet man talked his way onto a
passenger plane,

14
00:00:48,176 --> 00:00:51,787
threatened it with a bomb, collected a
fortune in cash,

15
00:00:51,854 --> 00:00:55,661
and walked out the back of the aircraft
into the dark sky.

16
00:00:55,728 --> 00:00:57,304
He was never seen again.

17
00:00:57,370 --> 00:01:01,572
Not his face, not his body, not the money
strapped to his chest.

18
00:01:01,639 --> 00:01:06,103
He simply stopped existing somewhere over
the forests of the Pacific

19
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:09,124
Northwest, and he has stayed gone ever
since.

20
00:01:13,974 --> 00:01:17,727
The world came to know him by a name that
was never even his.

21
00:01:17,794 --> 00:01:22,224
D.B. Cooper. Over the next fifty years
that name grew into something far

22
00:01:22,291 --> 00:01:23,829
stranger than a criminal.

23
00:01:23,896 --> 00:01:27,772
It became a legend, an unsolved itch the
country could not stop

24
00:01:27,839 --> 00:01:30,607
scratching. More than eight hundred
suspects.

25
00:01:30,674 --> 00:01:32,274
A forty-five-year manhunt.

26
00:01:32,341 --> 00:01:36,709
Books, songs, conventions, and a single
bundle of rotting twenty-dollar

27
00:01:36,776 --> 00:01:39,237
bills dug out of a riverbank by a child.

28
00:01:44,087 --> 00:01:46,641
None of it was ever enough to give him a
name.

29
00:01:51,491 --> 00:01:54,286
It helps to know the moment he stepped
into.

30
00:01:54,353 --> 00:01:58,418
The early 1970s were the golden age of the
airplane hijacking in

31
00:01:58,485 --> 00:02:00,708
America. There were dozens of them,

32
00:02:00,775 --> 00:02:05,158
year after year, so many that flight crews
were quietly trained to go

33
00:02:05,224 --> 00:02:07,574
along with it, to keep everyone calm,

34
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:12,088
hand over what was asked, and get the
plane safely back on the ground.

35
00:02:12,154 --> 00:02:15,013
Most of those hijackers wanted a
destination,

36
00:02:15,079 --> 00:02:18,192
usually Cuba, and a cause they could shout
about.

37
00:02:18,259 --> 00:02:20,101
This man wanted none of that.

38
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:22,581
No country, no manifesto, no audience.

39
00:02:22,648 --> 00:02:27,094
He wanted cash, and a parachute, and a way
to walk off the edge of the

40
00:02:27,161 --> 00:02:31,290
map. That alone set him apart before he
had done anything at all.

41
00:02:36,140 --> 00:02:39,397
Over the next half hour we are going to do
three things.

42
00:02:39,464 --> 00:02:43,012
First, we will walk through exactly what
happened that night,

43
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:47,150
minute by minute, because the calm of it
is somehow more chilling than

44
00:02:47,217 --> 00:02:48,904
any violence would have been.

45
00:02:48,971 --> 00:02:52,868
Then we will look at the only solid piece
of evidence the case ever

46
00:02:52,934 --> 00:02:55,610
produced, and what it does and does not
prove.

47
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:59,516
And finally we will sit with the question
that has outlived nearly

48
00:02:59,582 --> 00:03:01,211
everyone who ever chased it.

49
00:03:01,278 --> 00:03:03,895
Who was he, and did he even survive the
fall?

50
00:03:03,962 --> 00:03:08,034
After half a century, the honest answer is
also the most maddening one

51
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:09,438
there is. Nobody knows.

52
00:03:14,288 --> 00:03:17,120
He bought his ticket under the name Dan
Cooper.

53
00:03:17,186 --> 00:03:21,223
A short hop, Portland to Seattle, the kind
of late-afternoon flight

54
00:03:21,289 --> 00:03:23,157
nobody is supposed to remember.

55
00:03:23,224 --> 00:03:25,453
He paid cash. He carried a briefcase.

56
00:03:25,519 --> 00:03:28,351
He took a seat near the back of the Boeing
727,

57
00:03:28,418 --> 00:03:30,104
ordered a drink, and waited.

58
00:03:30,171 --> 00:03:33,906
By the descriptions that survive, he
looked like a middle-aged

59
00:03:33,973 --> 00:03:36,503
businessman in a dark suit and a thin tie.

60
00:03:36,570 --> 00:03:39,040
Forgettable, almost certainly on purpose.

61
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:47,884
The famous initials came later, and by
accident.

62
00:03:47,951 --> 00:03:52,479
In the chaos of that first night, a
wire-service report garbled the

63
00:03:52,546 --> 00:03:56,331
details, and the wrong name went out
across the country.

64
00:03:56,397 --> 00:04:00,250
It stuck. The man on the plane called
himself Dan Cooper.

65
00:04:00,316 --> 00:04:02,276
The world has called him D.B.

66
00:04:02,343 --> 00:04:06,263
Cooper ever since, which means that even
his fake name is,

67
00:04:06,330 --> 00:04:10,182
in a small way, a mistake nobody ever
managed to correct.

68
00:04:15,032 --> 00:04:18,495
Not long after takeoff, he handed a note
to one of the flight

69
00:04:18,561 --> 00:04:21,797
attendants. She was young, working the
back of the cabin,

70
00:04:21,864 --> 00:04:25,497
and her first instinct was to assume it
was a lonely businessman

71
00:04:25,564 --> 00:04:27,267
slipping her his phone number.

72
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:30,512
She tucked the paper into her pocket
without reading it.

73
00:04:30,579 --> 00:04:32,679
He leaned toward her, calm and quiet,

74
00:04:32,746 --> 00:04:35,017
and told her she had better take a look.

75
00:04:35,084 --> 00:04:36,673
The note said he had a bomb.

76
00:04:41,523 --> 00:04:44,956
He opened the briefcase just enough for
her to see inside.

77
00:04:45,023 --> 00:04:49,166
A nest of wires. Rows of red cylinders
that looked exactly like sticks

78
00:04:49,233 --> 00:04:51,601
of dynamite. Whether any of it was real,

79
00:04:51,668 --> 00:04:54,272
nobody on that airplane could possibly
know,

80
00:04:54,339 --> 00:04:56,825
and that uncertainty was the whole weapon.

81
00:04:56,891 --> 00:04:59,496
A bomb you cannot test is a bomb that
works.

82
00:05:04,346 --> 00:05:08,431
Then he told her what he wanted, and the
precision of the list is the

83
00:05:08,497 --> 00:05:10,865
first sign that this was not improvised.

84
00:05:10,932 --> 00:05:14,070
Two hundred thousand dollars, in
twenty-dollar bills.

85
00:05:14,136 --> 00:05:15,320
And four parachutes.

86
00:05:15,387 --> 00:05:17,163
Two main chutes, two reserves.

87
00:05:22,013 --> 00:05:26,344
Investigators have turned that parachute
detail over for fifty years.

88
00:05:26,411 --> 00:05:27,980
Four chutes, for one man.

89
00:05:28,047 --> 00:05:31,625
Why four? The leading idea is chilling in
its cleverness.

90
00:05:31,691 --> 00:05:36,085
By asking for four, he made the
authorities believe he might force one

91
00:05:36,152 --> 00:05:39,165
of the crew or passengers to jump
alongside him.

92
00:05:39,232 --> 00:05:42,684
And if an innocent person might be going
out that door,

93
00:05:42,751 --> 00:05:44,948
the parachutes could not be rigged,

94
00:05:45,014 --> 00:05:48,279
could not be sabotaged, could not be
packed to fail.

95
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:51,609
He had quietly guaranteed himself working
equipment,

96
00:05:51,676 --> 00:05:53,120
without ever saying so.

97
00:05:53,186 --> 00:05:54,316
That is not panic.

98
00:05:54,383 --> 00:05:55,450
That is planning.

99
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:03,233
What unsettles everyone who studies that
flight,

100
00:06:03,299 --> 00:06:04,888
though, is not the threat.

101
00:06:04,954 --> 00:06:07,276
It is the manner of the man making it.

102
00:06:07,343 --> 00:06:09,909
He was unfailingly polite the entire time.

103
00:06:09,975 --> 00:06:11,136
He lit a cigarette.

104
00:06:11,203 --> 00:06:14,257
He ordered a bourbon and soda and drank it
slowly.

105
00:06:14,324 --> 00:06:18,234
While the crew quietly understood that the
airplane was now his,

106
00:06:18,301 --> 00:06:21,905
the man at the center of it behaved like a
passenger mildly

107
00:06:21,972 --> 00:06:24,355
inconvenienced by a delayed connection.

108
00:06:24,421 --> 00:06:25,949
No shouting. No sweating.

109
00:06:26,015 --> 00:06:30,292
Just a calm, courteous stranger who
happened to be holding a planeload

110
00:06:30,359 --> 00:06:32,436
of people in the palm of his hand.

111
00:06:37,286 --> 00:06:41,563
The plane circled near Seattle while the
money and the parachutes were

112
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:43,830
gathered on the ground, then landed.

113
00:06:43,896 --> 00:06:46,585
There the exchange happened, fast and
tense.

114
00:06:46,652 --> 00:06:49,340
The cash and the chutes were carried
aboard.

115
00:06:49,407 --> 00:06:52,707
In return, he kept his word and let the
passengers go.

116
00:06:52,773 --> 00:06:55,523
About thirty-six of them filed off the
plane,

117
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:57,728
most of them never quite realizing,

118
00:06:57,795 --> 00:07:01,889
until they read about it later, how close
they had been to the most

119
00:07:01,956 --> 00:07:04,278
famous skyjacking in American history.

120
00:07:04,344 --> 00:07:05,933
He kept only a small crew.

121
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,627
The two pilots up front, a flight
engineer,

122
00:07:08,694 --> 00:07:10,222
and one flight attendant.

123
00:07:10,288 --> 00:07:13,649
Then the plane was refueled for the next,
stranger leg.

124
00:07:18,499 --> 00:07:22,746
None of this happened in a rush, and the
patience of it is part of what

125
00:07:22,812 --> 00:07:27,119
unnerves people. While the airline
scrambled on the ground to gather two

126
00:07:27,186 --> 00:07:31,134
hundred thousand dollars in twenties and
round up four parachutes,

127
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:33,653
the plane stayed in the air near Seattle,

128
00:07:33,719 --> 00:07:37,129
circling, burning fuel, for the better
part of two hours.

129
00:07:37,195 --> 00:07:41,203
Two full hours of a calm man in a window
seat with a lit cigarette,

130
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:44,200
and a cabin of passengers who, for the
most part,

131
00:07:44,267 --> 00:07:47,198
still had no idea that anything at all was
wrong.

132
00:07:52,048 --> 00:07:55,879
The flight attendant seated closest to him
spent much of that time at

133
00:07:55,946 --> 00:07:59,000
his side, carrying his demands to the
cockpit and back.

134
00:07:59,067 --> 00:08:01,565
By her account he was not a snarling
villain.

135
00:08:01,632 --> 00:08:03,520
He was composed, almost courteous,

136
00:08:03,587 --> 00:08:07,307
the particular kind of polite that is
somehow more frightening than

137
00:08:07,374 --> 00:08:11,261
shouting, because it tells you the man has
thought all of this through

138
00:08:11,327 --> 00:08:12,993
and is in no hurry whatsoever.

139
00:08:17,843 --> 00:08:20,016
Picture the ransom itself for a second.

140
00:08:20,083 --> 00:08:22,646
Two hundred thousand dollars, all in
twenties,

141
00:08:22,712 --> 00:08:24,830
is around ten thousand separate bills,

142
00:08:24,896 --> 00:08:27,515
and it weighs something close to twenty
pounds.

143
00:08:27,582 --> 00:08:29,476
He had it brought aboard in a bag,

144
00:08:29,543 --> 00:08:33,499
and by the accounts that survive, he
lashed that bag to his body before

145
00:08:33,566 --> 00:08:35,906
he jumped. A grown man in a business suit,

146
00:08:35,972 --> 00:08:39,594
about to step into a freezing storm at two
hundred miles an hour,

147
00:08:39,661 --> 00:08:42,781
with a twenty-pound brick of cash strapped
to his chest.

148
00:08:47,631 --> 00:08:51,782
His instructions to the cockpit were
unusual enough that the pilots had

149
00:08:51,849 --> 00:08:53,544
to push back on some of them.

150
00:08:53,611 --> 00:08:54,955
Fly toward Mexico City.

151
00:08:55,022 --> 00:08:57,010
Stay low, under ten thousand feet.

152
00:08:57,077 --> 00:08:59,649
Keep the speed down, the wing flaps
lowered,

153
00:08:59,716 --> 00:09:01,879
the landing gear hanging in the wind.

154
00:09:01,946 --> 00:09:05,278
And leave the rear staircase, the aft
airstair, unlocked.

155
00:09:10,128 --> 00:09:13,371
Notice what those demands quietly revealed
about him.

156
00:09:13,438 --> 00:09:17,660
This was not a man who had wandered onto
just any flight with a vague

157
00:09:17,727 --> 00:09:19,012
idea and a fake bomb.

158
00:09:19,078 --> 00:09:21,403
He had chosen a Boeing 727 on purpose,

159
00:09:21,470 --> 00:09:25,692
and he understood its one unusual feature
better than most passengers

160
00:09:25,759 --> 00:09:30,103
ever would. He knew it could be flown slow
and low with the rear stairs

161
00:09:30,170 --> 00:09:34,392
down. He knew a person could drop straight
out of the back of it into

162
00:09:34,459 --> 00:09:36,907
open air and clear the aircraft cleanly.

163
00:09:36,973 --> 00:09:39,054
Whoever he was, he knew airplanes,

164
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:43,281
and he had picked the exact machine that
turned an impossible escape

165
00:09:43,348 --> 00:09:44,633
into a workable plan.

166
00:09:49,483 --> 00:09:52,403
You have to understand the machine he had
chosen.

167
00:09:52,470 --> 00:09:55,986
The Boeing 727 carried a set of stairs
built into its tail,

168
00:09:56,053 --> 00:09:57,185
under the fuselage.

169
00:09:57,252 --> 00:10:01,006
On the ground, passengers could walk down
them onto the tarmac.

170
00:10:01,073 --> 00:10:04,231
In the air, with the right plane and the
right nerve,

171
00:10:04,298 --> 00:10:06,980
they could be lowered straight into open
sky.

172
00:10:07,047 --> 00:10:11,159
Up in the locked cockpit, the crew watched
a small warning light tell

173
00:10:11,225 --> 00:10:15,397
them that the back of their own airplane
had just opened to the night.

174
00:10:20,247 --> 00:10:24,612
What happened next is the entire reason we
are still talking about this man.

175
00:10:30,212 --> 00:10:33,995
Somewhere over the dark, wooded country of
southwestern Washington,

176
00:10:34,061 --> 00:10:36,094
a little after eight in the evening,

177
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:38,475
he made his way to the back of the cabin.

178
00:10:38,542 --> 00:10:42,494
He went down those open stairs into a
roaring wall of freezing air and

179
00:10:42,561 --> 00:10:45,609
rain, the plane moving near two hundred
miles an hour,

180
00:10:45,676 --> 00:10:47,370
the cash strapped to his body.

181
00:10:47,436 --> 00:10:48,509
And he stepped off.

182
00:10:53,359 --> 00:10:54,625
The crew felt him go.

183
00:10:54,692 --> 00:10:57,164
A subtle shift in the airplane's balance,

184
00:10:57,230 --> 00:10:59,582
a small bump as a weight left the tail.

185
00:10:59,648 --> 00:11:03,145
They called to him over the intercom and
got nothing back.

186
00:11:03,212 --> 00:11:07,372
He was already falling through the night
toward a forest he could not

187
00:11:07,439 --> 00:11:11,780
see, in conditions that would have
frightened a trained paratrooper with

188
00:11:11,847 --> 00:11:13,655
a map and a moonlit drop zone.

189
00:11:13,722 --> 00:11:14,722
He had neither.

190
00:11:19,572 --> 00:11:24,090
Hold on the cold for a moment, because the
cold is the heart of the

191
00:11:24,157 --> 00:11:28,877
gamble. He went out the back of that jet
in a business suit and a thin

192
00:11:28,943 --> 00:11:31,708
trench coat, into air far below freezing,

193
00:11:31,775 --> 00:11:34,674
into driving rain and darkness, over
steep,

194
00:11:34,741 --> 00:11:39,461
timbered terrain, at night, with no lights
below to aim for and no way

195
00:11:39,528 --> 00:11:44,181
to know whether he was about to land in a
clearing or in the top of a

196
00:11:44,247 --> 00:11:45,461
hundred-foot tree.

197
00:11:45,528 --> 00:11:50,383
Experienced skydivers turn down jumps in a
fraction of those conditions.

198
00:11:50,449 --> 00:11:52,405
He chose all of them at once.

199
00:11:57,255 --> 00:11:59,656
When the plane finally landed at Reno,

200
00:11:59,722 --> 00:12:01,365
federal agents swarmed it.

201
00:12:01,432 --> 00:12:03,011
The aft stairs were down.

202
00:12:03,078 --> 00:12:07,626
The man was gone. The cabin held only the
few things he had left behind.

203
00:12:07,693 --> 00:12:09,019
Some cigarette butts.

204
00:12:09,086 --> 00:12:11,739
The black clip-on tie he had been wearing,

205
00:12:11,806 --> 00:12:13,196
dropped near his seat.

206
00:12:13,263 --> 00:12:14,779
And almost nothing else.

207
00:12:14,845 --> 00:12:16,046
No wallet. No name.

208
00:12:16,112 --> 00:12:17,565
No useful fingerprints.

209
00:12:17,632 --> 00:12:22,054
A passenger who had sat among them for
hours had managed to leave less

210
00:12:22,121 --> 00:12:25,722
of himself than a stranger passing through
a hotel lobby.

211
00:12:30,572 --> 00:12:33,170
On the ground, the response was enormous.

212
00:12:33,237 --> 00:12:36,469
Once the plane was down and the man
confirmed gone,

213
00:12:36,536 --> 00:12:40,592
finding where he had landed became
something close to a military

214
00:12:40,659 --> 00:12:42,940
operation. Soldiers, federal agents,

215
00:12:43,007 --> 00:12:47,380
and local crews worked the rugged country
beneath the flight path for

216
00:12:47,447 --> 00:12:49,158
weeks, and then for months.

217
00:12:49,224 --> 00:12:50,555
Helicopters overhead.

218
00:12:50,622 --> 00:12:53,157
Lines of men on foot through the timber.

219
00:12:53,224 --> 00:12:55,125
They turned up nothing at all.

220
00:12:55,192 --> 00:12:58,297
No parachute, no footprint, no scrap of
the cash.

221
00:13:03,147 --> 00:13:05,558
Part of the problem was the land itself.

222
00:13:05,624 --> 00:13:09,542
The country below that flight path is
punishing even in daylight.

223
00:13:09,608 --> 00:13:11,959
Dense old-growth forest, steep ravines,

224
00:13:12,025 --> 00:13:16,304
cold fast rivers, the kind of terrain that
can hide a crashed airplane,

225
00:13:16,371 --> 00:13:17,757
let alone a single man.

226
00:13:17,824 --> 00:13:20,717
If he came down hurt, or came down in the
water,

227
00:13:20,783 --> 00:13:25,002
there were a hundred different ways for
the wilderness to take him and

228
00:13:25,069 --> 00:13:26,274
never give him back.

229
00:13:31,124 --> 00:13:34,171
There was also a darker detail buried in
the equipment.

230
00:13:34,238 --> 00:13:37,950
One of the parachutes the authorities
handed him turned out to be a

231
00:13:38,017 --> 00:13:40,731
training rig, sewn shut, useless for a
real jump.

232
00:13:40,798 --> 00:13:44,344
Nobody knows for certain which chute he
strapped on in the dark,

233
00:13:44,410 --> 00:13:46,627
or whether the one he chose ever opened.

234
00:13:46,693 --> 00:13:50,516
It is entirely possible that the most
famous skyjacker in history was

235
00:13:50,583 --> 00:13:53,297
dead within minutes of stepping off those
stairs,

236
00:13:53,364 --> 00:13:55,691
and that the forest simply kept the proof.

237
00:14:00,541 --> 00:14:03,255
This is the moment the case becomes the
case,

238
00:14:03,322 --> 00:14:06,157
because the jump has only two possible
endings,

239
00:14:06,223 --> 00:14:09,179
and both of them are unbearable in their
own way.

240
00:14:14,029 --> 00:14:15,644
Either he died that night.

241
00:14:15,711 --> 00:14:18,071
He hit the trees at speed in the dark,

242
00:14:18,138 --> 00:14:22,610
or the cold finished him, or he came down
in a river and was swept away,

243
00:14:22,677 --> 00:14:26,528
and his body and his money sank into a
wilderness so vast that

244
00:14:26,595 --> 00:14:29,453
searchers, even knowing roughly where to
look,

245
00:14:29,519 --> 00:14:31,010
never turned up a trace.

246
00:14:31,077 --> 00:14:33,313
For years, teams combed that ground.

247
00:14:33,380 --> 00:14:34,870
They found no parachute.

248
00:14:34,937 --> 00:14:36,490
No equipment. No remains.

249
00:14:36,557 --> 00:14:39,849
Not a single one of those marked
twenty-dollar bills.

250
00:14:44,699 --> 00:14:47,306
Or he lived. He came down through the
storm,

251
00:14:47,373 --> 00:14:49,447
gathered up his chute and his cash,

252
00:14:49,513 --> 00:14:51,765
walked out of those woods before dawn,

253
00:14:51,832 --> 00:14:54,261
and quietly returned to an ordinary life.

254
00:14:54,328 --> 00:14:55,690
A neighbor. A coworker.

255
00:14:55,757 --> 00:14:59,846
A man at the end of a bar, carrying the
secret of a perfect crime for

256
00:14:59,912 --> 00:15:04,297
the rest of his days while the whole
country argued about who he might be.

257
00:15:09,147 --> 00:15:13,388
Fifty years of investigation has never
been able to clearly favor one

258
00:15:13,454 --> 00:15:14,807
ending over the other.

259
00:15:14,873 --> 00:15:17,393
And that gap, that permanent uncertainty,

260
00:15:17,460 --> 00:15:20,717
is exactly what turned a hijacker into a
folk legend.

261
00:15:20,784 --> 00:15:23,857
A criminal we can picture dying alone in
the rain,

262
00:15:23,923 --> 00:15:27,119
or beating the entire system and getting
away clean.

263
00:15:27,186 --> 00:15:29,767
The country has never been able to choose,

264
00:15:29,834 --> 00:15:30,834
so it kept both.

265
00:15:35,684 --> 00:15:38,842
The FBI put a code name on the case almost
immediately.

266
00:15:38,908 --> 00:15:41,090
NORJAK, short for Northwest hijacking.

267
00:15:41,157 --> 00:15:45,233
What followed became one of the longest
and strangest investigations in

268
00:15:45,299 --> 00:15:46,505
the Bureau's history.

269
00:15:51,355 --> 00:15:55,579
In the first five years alone, agents
seriously examined more than eight

270
00:15:55,645 --> 00:15:58,579
hundred suspects. They ran down thousands
of tips.

271
00:15:58,645 --> 00:16:01,285
They pulled in skydivers and
ex-paratroopers,

272
00:16:01,352 --> 00:16:03,581
men with grudges and men with records,

273
00:16:03,648 --> 00:16:06,170
anyone who fit the loose outline of a
calm,

274
00:16:06,237 --> 00:16:09,287
middle-aged man who knew his way around a
parachute.

275
00:16:09,354 --> 00:16:12,346
One by one, the names were checked and
crossed off.

276
00:16:17,196 --> 00:16:20,537
It is worth remembering exactly when this
happened.

277
00:16:20,603 --> 00:16:23,682
1971. There was no DNA testing to fall
back on.

278
00:16:23,749 --> 00:16:26,238
No security cameras watching the gate,

279
00:16:26,305 --> 00:16:28,859
no body scanners, no database of faces,

280
00:16:28,926 --> 00:16:30,826
no digital trail of any kind.

281
00:16:30,892 --> 00:16:34,102
A passenger could pay in cash, give a
false name,

282
00:16:34,169 --> 00:16:38,623
sit quietly for a few hours, and walk away
having left behind almost

283
00:16:38,690 --> 00:16:41,375
nothing but a memory and a discarded tie.

284
00:16:41,442 --> 00:16:45,896
The tools that crack a modern case in days
simply did not exist yet,

285
00:16:45,963 --> 00:16:48,911
and by the time some of them finally
arrived,

286
00:16:48,977 --> 00:16:51,794
the trail had already gone cold by
decades.

287
00:16:56,644 --> 00:17:00,564
And the harder they looked, the clearer
one cruel fact became.

288
00:17:00,631 --> 00:17:03,350
The man had been clever in a very
specific,

289
00:17:03,417 --> 00:17:07,401
very modern way. He had given them almost
nothing to work with.

290
00:17:07,467 --> 00:17:11,641
The description was generic, a suit and a
tie and dark sunglasses.

291
00:17:11,707 --> 00:17:13,731
There was no usable fingerprint.

292
00:17:13,797 --> 00:17:18,287
There was no photograph, because in 1971
there was no camera pointed at

293
00:17:18,354 --> 00:17:19,935
a man in seat eighteen-E.

294
00:17:20,001 --> 00:17:24,111
All the FBI ever really had was a
composite sketch built from the

295
00:17:24,178 --> 00:17:26,265
frightened memories of strangers.

296
00:17:26,331 --> 00:17:28,039
And a sketch is not a face.

297
00:17:28,105 --> 00:17:31,520
It is a guess that everyone agrees to
treat as a face.

298
00:17:36,370 --> 00:17:40,394
So the search widened, and into that gap
rushed the suspects people

299
00:17:40,461 --> 00:17:41,902
still argue about today.

300
00:17:41,969 --> 00:17:46,053
Over the decades, a long parade of names
has been put forward as the

301
00:17:46,120 --> 00:17:50,324
real D.B. Cooper. A skydiver who pulled a
strikingly similar hijacking

302
00:17:50,391 --> 00:17:51,472
just months later.

303
00:17:51,538 --> 00:17:53,100
A former airline employee.

304
00:17:53,167 --> 00:17:54,428
An ex-soldier or two.

305
00:17:54,495 --> 00:17:57,978
Men named by their own relatives,
sometimes on a deathbed.

306
00:17:58,045 --> 00:18:01,829
Men named by amateur investigators working
a wall of string and

307
00:18:01,895 --> 00:18:03,757
photographs in a spare bedroom.

308
00:18:03,824 --> 00:18:07,067
Men named by authors with a theory and a
book to sell.

309
00:18:11,917 --> 00:18:15,201
A few of those names carried real weight
behind them.

310
00:18:15,268 --> 00:18:19,234
Only months after Cooper, a man named
Richard McCoy pulled off a

311
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:23,701
strikingly similar hijacking, demanding
cash and parachutes and leaping

312
00:18:23,767 --> 00:18:25,812
from the back of a jet in flight.

313
00:18:25,879 --> 00:18:28,048
McCoy was caught for his own crime,

314
00:18:28,115 --> 00:18:32,576
and for years he sat near the very top of
the list of men who might also

315
00:18:32,643 --> 00:18:36,671
have been Cooper. The FBI examined him
closely and ruled him out,

316
00:18:36,738 --> 00:18:40,952
concluding he simply did not match the
descriptions from Flight 305.

317
00:18:41,018 --> 00:18:43,187
He is this whole case in miniature.

318
00:18:43,254 --> 00:18:45,485
A vivid suspect, a satisfying story,

319
00:18:45,552 --> 00:18:49,022
and then the evidence quietly refusing to
close the gap.

320
00:18:54,622 --> 00:18:58,248
Other names were offered up by the people
closest to them.

321
00:18:58,314 --> 00:19:01,752
A widow who said her husband confessed on
his deathbed.

322
00:19:01,819 --> 00:19:05,632
A niece who recognized something in an old
family photograph.

323
00:19:05,699 --> 00:19:08,137
A brother, a friend, a former coworker,

324
00:19:08,203 --> 00:19:12,579
each one certain, each holding a piece
that almost fit and never quite

325
00:19:12,646 --> 00:19:16,771
did. Whole books and documentaries have
been built around a single

326
00:19:16,838 --> 00:19:21,151
candidate. Not one of them has ever
crossed the line from a confident

327
00:19:21,218 --> 00:19:22,718
story into actual proof.

328
00:19:27,568 --> 00:19:29,796
Here is the part that matters most,

329
00:19:29,863 --> 00:19:32,855
and the part that most retellings quietly
skip.

330
00:19:32,922 --> 00:19:35,787
The FBI never confirmed a single one of
them.

331
00:19:35,854 --> 00:19:40,247
Not one. Every name you have ever heard
attached to this case was put

332
00:19:40,313 --> 00:19:43,242
there by someone other than the Bureau
itself.

333
00:19:43,309 --> 00:19:46,237
Some of those proposed suspects are long
dead.

334
00:19:46,304 --> 00:19:48,341
The status of others is murkier.

335
00:19:48,408 --> 00:19:52,419
And a theory, however confident, however
slickly presented on a

336
00:19:52,485 --> 00:19:54,841
late-night special, is not a verdict.

337
00:19:54,908 --> 00:19:56,690
No court has ever named D.B.

338
00:19:56,757 --> 00:19:59,113
Cooper. No agency has ever named D.B.

339
00:19:59,179 --> 00:20:04,209
Cooper. After everything, the chair at the
center of this story is still empty.

340
00:20:09,059 --> 00:20:13,063
Over the years, the hunt curdled into a
kind of national hobby,

341
00:20:13,129 --> 00:20:14,909
and it never really stopped.

342
00:20:14,975 --> 00:20:17,581
Retired engineers, amateur investigators,

343
00:20:17,648 --> 00:20:22,033
armchair detectives, and entire
conventions of enthusiasts have spent

344
00:20:22,099 --> 00:20:25,150
decades picking over every fragment of the
case.

345
00:20:25,217 --> 00:20:29,411
When investigators lifted microscopic
particles off that abandoned

346
00:20:29,478 --> 00:20:31,956
clip-on tie, the hobbyists ran with it,

347
00:20:32,023 --> 00:20:36,535
arguing that traces of rare metals pointed
to a man who had once worked

348
00:20:36,602 --> 00:20:38,572
in aerospace or heavy industry.

349
00:20:38,638 --> 00:20:43,023
Every few years a fresh team steps forward
to announce it has finally

350
00:20:43,090 --> 00:20:45,251
solved the thing. Every few years,

351
00:20:45,317 --> 00:20:48,114
closer scrutiny lets the air right back
out.

352
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:50,532
The search has now outlasted careers,

353
00:20:50,598 --> 00:20:55,174
marriages, and the lifetimes of many of
the people who first took it up.

354
00:21:00,024 --> 00:21:03,115
The case did leave one permanent mark on
the world,

355
00:21:03,181 --> 00:21:06,817
though, and you have benefited from it
without ever knowing.

356
00:21:06,884 --> 00:21:10,944
After Cooper, and after a short rash of
copycats who tried the same

357
00:21:11,011 --> 00:21:15,192
trick, the government required a simple
device on airliners with rear

358
00:21:15,259 --> 00:21:18,652
stairways. A small hinged vane, mounted by
the airstair,

359
00:21:18,719 --> 00:21:21,688
that the rushing air of flight pushes into
place,

360
00:21:21,755 --> 00:21:25,876
locking the stairs shut so they cannot be
lowered while the plane is

361
00:21:25,943 --> 00:21:28,548
moving. They still call it the Cooper
vane.

362
00:21:28,615 --> 00:21:31,706
It is named for a man whose real name
nobody knows,

363
00:21:31,772 --> 00:21:35,711
and its only job is to make certain that
no one can ever again do

364
00:21:35,778 --> 00:21:36,990
exactly what he did.

365
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:45,029
For nine years, that was the shape of the
case.

366
00:21:45,096 --> 00:21:49,100
A vivid crime, a vanished man, a mountain
of dead-end tips,

367
00:21:49,166 --> 00:21:54,052
and not one dollar of the ransom ever
surfacing in a bank or a shop or a

368
00:21:54,119 --> 00:21:56,155
casino, anywhere in the world.

369
00:21:56,221 --> 00:21:59,275
The money was as gone as the man who took
it.

370
00:21:59,342 --> 00:22:03,481
And then, on a riverbank, a little boy
reached into the sand.

371
00:22:08,331 --> 00:22:12,681
In 1980, a family stopped for a cookout on
the bank of the Columbia

372
00:22:12,748 --> 00:22:15,475
River, at a sandy stretch called Tena Bar.

373
00:22:15,541 --> 00:22:18,658
A boy about eight years old, named Brian
Ingram,

374
00:22:18,725 --> 00:22:21,971
was smoothing out a patch of sand to build
a fire.

375
00:22:22,038 --> 00:22:26,583
Just beneath the surface, his fingers
closed on something that was not

376
00:22:26,649 --> 00:22:27,883
sand. It was paper.

377
00:22:32,733 --> 00:22:35,929
It was money. Decaying, water-rotted
twenty-dollar bills,

378
00:22:35,996 --> 00:22:38,182
still grouped in rubber-banded bundles,

379
00:22:38,249 --> 00:22:40,548
about fifty-eight hundred dollars in all.

380
00:22:40,615 --> 00:22:44,483
The FBI checked the serial numbers against
the list they had kept for

381
00:22:44,550 --> 00:22:46,232
nearly a decade. They matched.

382
00:22:46,299 --> 00:22:48,205
After nine years of total silence,

383
00:22:48,272 --> 00:22:51,692
a piece of D.B. Cooper's ransom had come
back into the world,

384
00:22:51,759 --> 00:22:55,684
out of a riverbank, in the hands of a
child who had not been born when

385
00:22:55,751 --> 00:22:56,928
the crime took place.

386
00:23:01,778 --> 00:23:04,995
And here is the cruel joke at the center
of this case.

387
00:23:05,062 --> 00:23:09,232
That discovery, the single hardest piece
of physical evidence in fifty

388
00:23:09,298 --> 00:23:11,860
years of searching, did not solve
anything.

389
00:23:11,927 --> 00:23:14,131
If anything, it deepened the mystery.

390
00:23:18,981 --> 00:23:23,200
Because no one has ever been able to fully
explain how the money got

391
00:23:23,267 --> 00:23:27,672
there. The spot was a long way from where
investigators believed Cooper

392
00:23:27,739 --> 00:23:30,407
had jumped. The bills were buried in a
way,

393
00:23:30,474 --> 00:23:34,941
and worn down in a way, that experts have
been arguing about ever since.

394
00:23:35,008 --> 00:23:39,413
Did the river slowly carry the cash there
over the years and bury it in

395
00:23:39,480 --> 00:23:42,334
the sand? Did Cooper come down near the
water,

396
00:23:42,401 --> 00:23:44,138
alive, and stash it himself?

397
00:23:44,205 --> 00:23:47,431
Did he die upstream and let the current do
the rest?

398
00:23:47,498 --> 00:23:50,910
Every answer you reach for creates a
brand-new problem.

399
00:23:50,977 --> 00:23:54,017
The one solid clue the case ever produced
points,

400
00:23:54,084 --> 00:23:56,876
infuriatingly, in several directions at
once.

401
00:24:01,726 --> 00:24:03,140
Then there was the tie.

402
00:24:03,207 --> 00:24:05,542
That black clip-on, left on the plane,

403
00:24:05,609 --> 00:24:07,822
sat in an evidence file for decades.

404
00:24:07,889 --> 00:24:12,192
As forensic science advanced,
investigators went back to it and lifted

405
00:24:12,258 --> 00:24:14,594
microscopic particles from its fibers,

406
00:24:14,661 --> 00:24:16,628
and later a partial DNA profile.

407
00:24:16,695 --> 00:24:21,059
For a moment it felt as though modern
science might finally finish what

408
00:24:21,126 --> 00:24:23,585
fifty years of detective work could not.

409
00:24:23,651 --> 00:24:28,077
It didn't. The particles fueled fresh
theories about where the man might

410
00:24:28,144 --> 00:24:31,402
have worked, in industry, around certain
rare metals.

411
00:24:31,469 --> 00:24:34,051
The DNA matched no one anyone could prove.

412
00:24:34,117 --> 00:24:37,744
Like the money and the sketch and the
whole maddening case,

413
00:24:37,811 --> 00:24:40,700
the tie offered just enough to keep hope
alive,

414
00:24:40,766 --> 00:24:42,918
and never enough to close the file.

415
00:24:47,768 --> 00:24:51,100
And the money only deepened the puzzle in
a second way.

416
00:24:51,166 --> 00:24:55,043
The bills at Tena Bar came to about
fifty-eight hundred dollars.

417
00:24:55,110 --> 00:24:58,260
That leaves the overwhelming majority of
the ransom,

418
00:24:58,327 --> 00:25:01,234
well over a hundred and ninety thousand
dollars,

419
00:25:01,301 --> 00:25:02,936
completely unaccounted for.

420
00:25:03,003 --> 00:25:05,184
Out of ten thousand marked twenties,

421
00:25:05,251 --> 00:25:07,492
not one other note has ever surfaced.

422
00:25:07,559 --> 00:25:10,527
Not in a bank, not in a shop, not in
circulation,

423
00:25:10,593 --> 00:25:13,501
not in an evidence locker anywhere in the
world.

424
00:25:13,568 --> 00:25:17,869
If he lived and spent it, he did so
without ever once tripping a serial

425
00:25:17,935 --> 00:25:21,994
number. If he died, the rest of it rotted
into the ground with him.

426
00:25:26,844 --> 00:25:30,498
The few bills they did find became their
own small battlefield.

427
00:25:30,564 --> 00:25:34,624
Some experts argued the river had carried
the cash downstream over the

428
00:25:34,691 --> 00:25:37,707
years and buried it in the sand long after
the jump.

429
00:25:37,773 --> 00:25:41,717
Others insisted the way the money was
layered and worn pointed to it

430
00:25:41,784 --> 00:25:45,032
being placed there on purpose, which would
mean someone,

431
00:25:45,098 --> 00:25:48,926
possibly Cooper himself, was alive and
moving near that riverbank.

432
00:25:48,993 --> 00:25:52,530
The single hardest clue in the entire case
did not narrow the

433
00:25:52,597 --> 00:25:54,569
possibilities. It multiplied them.

434
00:25:59,419 --> 00:26:03,258
By 2016, the FBI had carried NORJAK for
forty-five years.

435
00:26:03,324 --> 00:26:06,220
And that year, quietly, it made a
decision.

436
00:26:06,287 --> 00:26:08,846
It suspended the active investigation.

437
00:26:14,446 --> 00:26:17,951
Listen to that word, because the Bureau
chose it with care.

438
00:26:18,018 --> 00:26:19,325
Suspended. Not solved.

439
00:26:19,392 --> 00:26:22,006
To actually solve a case, the FBI
explained,

440
00:26:22,072 --> 00:26:25,994
you have to be able to prove who did it
beyond a reasonable doubt,

441
00:26:26,060 --> 00:26:28,080
and after four and a half decades,

442
00:26:28,147 --> 00:26:30,464
none of the tips, none of the suspects,

443
00:26:30,531 --> 00:26:33,917
none of the new technology had ever
delivered that proof.

444
00:26:33,984 --> 00:26:38,261
So the agents who had given pieces of
their careers to this thing set it

445
00:26:38,328 --> 00:26:41,299
down at last and turned to cases they
could close.

446
00:26:41,365 --> 00:26:43,385
The file, sixty-six volumes thick,

447
00:26:43,452 --> 00:26:44,878
was boxed and preserved.

448
00:26:44,945 --> 00:26:49,044
The answer to the only question that ever
mattered was not inside it.

449
00:26:53,894 --> 00:26:57,088
So we are left standing exactly where that
flight crew stood,

450
00:26:57,155 --> 00:27:00,873
staring at a little warning light that
says the back of the airplane is

451
00:27:00,940 --> 00:27:03,925
open, and at a space in the cabin where a
man used to be.

452
00:27:08,775 --> 00:27:12,836
It is worth asking why this one, out of
all the crimes that go unsolved,

453
00:27:12,902 --> 00:27:13,974
grew into a legend.

454
00:27:14,041 --> 00:27:17,481
People did not write songs about him
because he hurt someone.

455
00:27:17,547 --> 00:27:21,495
He didn't. The uncomfortable truth is that
a large part of the country

456
00:27:21,562 --> 00:27:23,367
half wanted him to have made it.

457
00:27:23,433 --> 00:27:26,761
A polite man in a business suit who
outsmarted the airline,

458
00:27:26,827 --> 00:27:29,534
the FBI, and the weather itself, took the
money,

459
00:27:29,601 --> 00:27:32,929
and melted into the rain like something
out of a tall tale.

460
00:27:32,995 --> 00:27:35,308
If he died in those woods, it is a small,

461
00:27:35,374 --> 00:27:39,040
lonely tragedy. If he lived, he pulled off
the only perfect crime

462
00:27:39,107 --> 00:27:41,701
America ever got to watch unfold in real
time.

463
00:27:46,551 --> 00:27:51,294
And maybe that is the real reason the case
refuses to let go of us.

464
00:27:51,361 --> 00:27:53,413
We are built to need endings.

465
00:27:53,480 --> 00:27:55,462
The confession. The verdict.

466
00:27:55,529 --> 00:27:56,874
The body. The name.

467
00:27:56,941 --> 00:28:00,622
This story declines to hand us a single
one of them.

468
00:28:00,688 --> 00:28:04,511
It just leaves the door open, the cold air
pouring in,

469
00:28:04,578 --> 00:28:08,471
and a question with no floor underneath
it. Who was he.

470
00:28:13,321 --> 00:28:17,637
There is something almost unbearable about
a story that refuses to end.

471
00:28:17,704 --> 00:28:21,169
We have built whole institutions on the
opposite promise.

472
00:28:21,236 --> 00:28:22,755
That the truth comes out.

473
00:28:22,822 --> 00:28:26,105
That patience and evidence and science
eventually win.

474
